
class Solution {
    public void rotate(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        
        // Create a temporary array to store rotated elements
        int[] temp = new int[n];
        
        // Place each element in its new position
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // (i + k) % n ensures wrapping around the array
            // Example: if index goes beyond array size, it comes back to start
            temp[(i + k) % n] = nums[i];
        }
        
        // Copy rotated elements back to original array
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = temp[i];
        }
    }
}
🧠 Key Concept

👉 (i + k) % n = new index after rotation

i = current index
k = steps to rotate
% n = wrap around if it exceeds array size
🔍 Example 1
Input:
nums = [1,2,3,4,5,6,7], k = 3
Step-by-step:
i	nums[i]	New Index (i+k)%n	temp array
0	1	(0+3)%7 = 3	[,,,1,,,]
1	2	4	[,,,1,2,,_]
2	3	5	[,,,1,2,3,]
3	4	6	[,,_,1,2,3,4]
4	5	0	[5,,,1,2,3,4]
5	6	1	[5,6,_,1,2,3,4]
6	7	2	[5,6,7,1,2,3,4]
Final Output:
[5,6,7,1,2,3,4]
